The 502s are fixed. nginx was sending requests to an old IP address for the php container.

**Cause:** Each deploy recreates the php container, and Docker gives it a new IP. nginx looked up `php` once when it started and kept using that address. After the last deploy the container moved from `.5` to `.7`, and nginx was still sending requests to `.5`. I confirmed this with `docker inspect`. I also ruled out php-fpm crashing, because its logs were clean.

**Fix:** In the nginx config, I replaced the `set $php_fpm php:9000` / `fastcgi_pass $php_fpm` setup with an upstream block that keeps looking up the name:

```nginx
resolver 127.0.0.11 valid=1s;   # Docker's internal DNS

upstream php_fpm {
    zone php_fpm 64k;
    server php:9000 resolve;
}
```

nginx now picks up the new IP within about a second of the container being recreated.

**Verified:** I recreated the php container twice. There were no 502s either time, and I didn't have to reload nginx.

The `resolve` option on upstream servers needs nginx 1.27.3 or newer, or NGINX Plus. Your current image supports it. If you ever pin or downgrade the nginx image, keep it at 1.27.3 or above, or this config will fail to load.

